    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I see. You're making a distinction between \"cherry-picking\" history and relying on the historical record itself. However, if both methodologies—originalism and living constitutionalism—rely on a scholar's or judge's interpretation of what is \"relevant\" or \"historical,\" isn't the distinction between them effectively a distinction without a difference? Many argue that both are essentially interpretive exercises driven by the individual's underlying political philosophy. If the end result is the same—a judge deciding how the law applies today—why is one considered \"objective\" and the other \"subjective\"?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
